{"qid":"Q107299","names":["Kingdom of Northumbria"],"title":"Kingdom of Northumbria","endonym":{"native":"Norþhymbra rīce","lang":"ang"},"capsule":"The Kingdom of Northumbria (Norþhymbra rīce) was formed through the union of Bernicia and Deira in the early 7th century, with Æthelfrith (r. c. 593–616) the first king to rule both. At its apex under Edwin (r. 616–633) and Oswald (r. 634–642), Northumbria dominated northern Britain from the Humber to the Firth of Forth and fostered the Hiberno-Saxon monastic culture associated with Lindisfarne and the Venerable Bede. Sustained Viking raiding began in 793 with the sack of Lindisfarne; Danes captured York (Jórvík) in 866 and installed client kings. The kingdom's independence ended in 927 when Æthelstan of Wessex expelled the Hiberno-Norse king Sihtric's successor and received the submission of northern rulers, though a Scandinavian line at York persisted until Eric Bloodaxe's expulsion and death in 954.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Northumbria's broad territorial outline is well attested through Bede's Ecclesiastical History and Anglo-Saxon charters, but sub-regional boundaries and the exact northern frontier toward Pictish territory remain uncertain.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Northumbria","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northumbria"},{"label":"Britannica: Northumbria","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Northumbria"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Bernicia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Bernicia"}],"britannica":"place/Northumbria","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Northumbria","rulers":[{"qid":"Q272196","name":"